170-pub enum IncomingFilterOutcome {
171-    /// Accept the connection.
172-    Accept,
173-    /// Tell the remote to retry with a token (a QUIC `RETRY` packet).
174-    ///
175-    /// What this does depends on the connection type:
176-    ///
177-    /// - **Direct (UDP) connections**: this is QUIC source address
178-    ///   validation. If the socket address was spoofed, the retry token is
179-    ///   sent to the spoofed address, so we never hear from the attacker
180-    ///   again. If the address was real, the client repeats the connection
181-    ///   attempt with the token, and the next [`Incoming`] for that flow has
182-    ///   [`Incoming::remote_addr_validated`] set to `true`. The token is
183-    ///   bound to the source address.
184-    ///
185-    /// - **Relay connections**: there is no source address to validate
186-    ///   (the relay already vouches for the packet origin), so the
187-    ///   "validation" itself has no security meaning. However, the retry
188-    ///   still imposes a real cost on the client: an extra round trip
189-    ///   through the relay plus the work of sending a fresh ClientHello
190-    ///   with the token. This filters out adversarial clients that
191-    ///   don't bother to handle retry tokens, and adds latency before
192-    ///   we get to the more expensive part of the handshake. The next
193-    ///   [`Incoming`] for that flow will also have
194-    ///   [`Incoming::remote_addr_validated`] set to `true`, but again,
195-    ///   that just means the client cooperated with the retry.
196-    ///
197-    /// In short: for direct connections, `Retry` is address validation;
198-    /// for relay connections, it is a cost-imposition mechanism.
199-    ///
